# Measurement session -- NOT therapeutic. Shaped to make every fault class the
# analyzer looks for visible, and to be short enough to iterate on.
#
# RIG (one audio interface, so every input shares one ADC clock):
#   ch1  device headphone jack -> line in      (NOT a microphone: a room adds
#                                               reverb and ~3 ms per metre, all
#                                               of it noise in a timing measurement)
#   ch2  solar cell / photodiode on one LED    (load it with 220R-1k: a bare
#                                               cell into a 1M Hi-Z input rolls
#                                               off at a couple of Hz and would
#                                               smear the very edges we time)
#
#   python3 tools/avmeasure/analyze.py analyze \
#       --ledc sessions/library/90_measure_sync.ledc --wav rec.wav \
#       --map "audioL=1,light1=2" --sync-tone 3000
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------- sync marker
# 600 ms of full-brightness white on every channel plus a 3 kHz tone. Both
# edges are sharp, which is the whole point: without a marker the analyzer can
# only cross-correlate, and on a session that fades in slowly it localises t=0
# to +/-2 s and says so rather than guessing. With it: +/-5 ms.
0     40 50 100 255 255 255 15   # all four zones, 100% bright
A 0   3000 0 70 0 1              # 3 kHz marker tone, ch1
600   0 50 0 0 0 0 15            # freq 0 => stop flicker
A 600 0 0 0 0 1                  # freq 0 => stop the tone

# ------------------------------------------------------- phase 1: t = 1..60 s
# Inner and outer run at DIFFERENT rates on purpose. One sensor sees one zone,
# so distinct rates tell us which zone it is actually watching -- that settles
# the inner/outer channel-map question as a side effect of any capture.
#   mask 9 = 1+8 = inner-left + inner-right
#   mask 6 = 2+4 = outer-left + outer-right
1000  10 50 80 0 0 255 9         # inner: 10 Hz, blue
1000   7 50 80 255 80 0 6        # outer:  7 Hz, amber
A 1000 240 0 60 0 1 250 0        # binaural 240/250 => 10 Hz beat, matches inner

# ------------------------------------------------------ phase 2: t = 60..120 s
# A hard step on all three quantities at one instant. Lateness, drops and
# audio-vs-light offset all become measurable here because nothing ramps.
60000 14 50 80 0 255 100 9       # inner -> 14 Hz, green
60000  9 50 80 255 0 120 6       # outer ->  9 Hz, magenta
A 60000 240 0 60 0 1 254 0       # beat -> 14 Hz, still matching inner

# ----------------------------------------------------- phase 3: t = 120..300 s
# Back to the phase-1 values. If the first and third blocks measure differently,
# that is drift rather than a one-off error -- the comparison is self-referencing.
120000 10 50 80 0 0 255 9
120000  7 50 80 255 80 0 6
A 120000 240 0 60 0 1 250 0

# 5 minutes total: long enough for a ppm-scale clock divergence to show as a
# growing lateness, short enough to re-record while iterating.
300000 0 50 0 0 0 0 15
A 300000 0 0 0 0 1
